  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "Tests extraction after unlearning using a new paired-checkpoint guidance attack, rather than the attacks or interventions named in the claim.",
    "gate_note": "Reports extraction outcomes for models after unlearning and compares post-unlearning generation with extraction using both pre- and post-unlearning checkpoints. The attack requires access to the earlier checkpoint or saved logits; it is not fine-tuning, activation ablation, quantization, or prompting-only recovery. Rows below record the strict extraction metric A-ESR1.0. Other metrics and experiments are noted in rows_left_out.",
    "test_reason": "Does not test any version. The attack needs the pre-unlearning checkpoint or its saved logits, which still hold the forgotten data. It steers the post-unlearning model toward the original model's outputs. The study never tries to recover knowledge from the unlearned model alone, so it cannot show the unlearned model hides rather than deletes. Unlearning here is mostly exact retraining without the forget set. The unlearned model's own extraction is near zero everywhere (A-ESR1.0 0–0.013). The finding is checkpoint-differencing privacy leakage, a related but different construct; it is recorded under fits_no_version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. Add three points. Exact unlearning can increase leakage when an attacker holds earlier checkpoints or logits. Gains are smaller under approximate unlearning because degraded utility distorts the guidance signal. Adding unrelated data or noisy gradients mitigates leakage only slightly or at severe utility cost.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "A paired-checkpoint model-guidance and token-filtering extraction attack, rather than fine-tuning, activation edits, quantization, or prompt-only attacks.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Extraction gains vary with dataset and model, the number of training epochs, guidance scale, token-filter strength, and—under approximate unlearning—the post-unlearning model's utility. The authors report that increasing unrelated data only slightly reduces extraction; noisy updates reduce it at substantial utility cost.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Guiding the post-unlearning model with the pre-unlearning checkpoint extracts more forget-set text than the pre-unlearning model alone. For exact unlearning, A-ESR1.0 rises e.g. 0.101→0.224 (Phi, MUSE) and 0.140→0.210 (medical). Under GA/NPO approximate unlearning the gains are smaller (0.070→0.073–0.090, Table 3).",
        "proposed_claim": "With access to both pre- and post-unlearning checkpoints or logits, an adversary can extract forgotten data beyond what the original model yields, even after exact unlearning.",
